{"name":"Granular Limestone","hex":"#e3e0da","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"granular","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Consisting of, or resembling, granules or grains.","Grainy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/granular"},{"term":"limestone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An abundant rock of marine and freshwater sediments; primarily composed of calcite (CaCO3); and occurring in a variety of forms, both crystalline and amorphous."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/limestone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Limestone","blocks":[{"text":"Limestone is a type of carbonate sedimentary rock which is the main source of the material lime. It is composed mostly of the minerals calcite and aragonite, which are different crystal forms of calcium carbonate CaCO3. Limestone forms when these minerals precipitate out of water containing dissolved calcium. This can take place through both biological and nonbiological processes, though biological processes, such as the accumulation of corals and shells in the sea, have likely been more important for the last 540 million years. Limestone often contains fossils which provide scientists with information on ancient environments and on the evolution of life."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/ElTorcal0408.jpg/330px-ElTorcal0408.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limestone","wikidata":"Q23757"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/ElTorcal0408.jpg/500px-ElTorcal0408.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/ElTorcal0408.jpg/1280px-ElTorcal0408.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/ElTorcal0408.jpg/500px-ElTorcal04